node_modules/
# npm rotates a debug log to `npm-debug.log.1`, `npm-debug.log.2026-10-17` or
# `npm-debug.log.gz`: the name is kept and the ending is not `.log`, so the
# `*.[Ll][Oo][Gg]` rule below misses exactly the logs most likely to be the long
# ones. The three spellings are character classes for the reason given at `.env`
# further down: ignore matching follows `core.ignorecase`, so on a case-sensitive
# checkout a single-case pattern covers only the lowercase name — and npm's debug
# log records the whole command line, the token in it included.
*.[Ll][Oo][Gg]
*.[Ll][Oo][Gg].*
[Nn][Pp][Mm]-[Dd][Ee][Bb][Uu][Gg].[Ll][Oo][Gg]*
.DS_Store
# `npm pack` writes the tarball it reports into the working directory.
*.tgz

# Working notes for this fork's own review rounds, kept out of the tree.
/zenfree-audit/

# Secrets. Nothing here is written by this repository, and none of it belongs in
# a commit: the npm token MAINTAINING.md describes lives in an npmrc outside it.
# The log patterns above are deliberately unanchored, because npm's debug logs
# record the whole command line and a log written from a subdirectory is the
# same leak. `*.[Ll][Oo][Gg].*` and `[Nn][Pp][Mm]-[Dd][Ee][Bb][Uu][Gg].[Ll][Oo][Gg]*`
# are that rule extended to the names a rotation leaves, because npm renames a
# log rather than rewriting it — a rotated log still holds the command line, it
# has just stopped ending in `.log`.
#
# Those, and the four names under them, are character classes — `.[Ee][Nn][Vv]`
# rather than `.env` — for the reason the key patterns further down state: on a
# case-sensitive checkout a single-case pattern ignores only the lowercase name,
# so `A.ENV` or `X.NPMRC`, the token file this project's own publish recipe
# writes, was left to be committed.
.[Ee][Nn][Vv]
.[Ee][Nn][Vv].*
# The template is the one file here a reader is meant to copy, and it is not a
# secret: it is put back in every spelling the two classes above can match. The
# literal `!.env.example` this replaces no longer covers them, because the class
# on the line above also matches `.ENV.EXAMPLE` and `.Env.Example`.
!.[Ee][Nn][Vv].[Ee][Xx][Aa][Mm][Pp][Ll][Ee]
.[Nn][Pp][Mm][Rr][Cc]
# The publish recipe in MAINTAINING.md writes its token to a file named `npmrc`,
# which `.[Nn][Pp][Mm][Rr][Cc]` does not match. This dotless spelling is kept
# beside it, and carries the same case coverage, so neither form is narrowed.
[Nn][Pp][Mm][Rr][Cc]
# Every secret pattern below is spelled as a double-written character class,
# `*.[Pp][Ee][Mm]` rather than `*.pem`. `.gitignore` matching is case-sensitive
# and follows `core.ignorecase`, so on a case-sensitive checkout (Linux CI, a
# macOS volume) the single-case spellings ignore only the lowercase names and
# let `CERT.PEM`, `KEY.P12` and `ID_RSA` through. This repository is published to
# npm, so a checkout that ignores half the keys is not good enough. The classes
# stay one extension per line: nothing here matches a file without the exact
# extension, and `LICENSE`, `NOTICE` and `.env.example` are untouched.
*.[Pp][Ee][Mm]
*.[Kk][Ee][Yy]
*.[Pp]12
*.[Pp][Ff][Xx]
*.[Jj][Kk][Ss]
*.[Kk][Ee][Yy][Ss][Tt][Oo][Rr][Ee]
*.[Dd][Ee][Rr]
[Ii][Dd]_[Rr][Ss][Aa]*
[Ii][Dd]_[Ee][Dd]25519*
[Ii][Dd]_[Ee][Cc][Dd][Ss][Aa]*
[Ii][Dd]_[Dd][Ss][Aa]*
*.[Pp][Pp][Kk]

# The public halves are not secrets, and a fixture or a deploy note legitimately
# carries one: the `id_*` lines above swallow them, so they are put back by name.
# These are written as classes too, and not only for consistency: the excludes
# above now cover every case, so a literal `!id_rsa.pub` would put back just the
# lowercase name and leave `ID_RSA.PUB` ignored. The matching set is widened on
# purpose — dropping an ignored public key only ever covers a publishing fixture,
# and it cannot un-ignore a private one, which none of these names can spell.
![Ii][Dd]_[Rr][Ss][Aa].[Pp][Uu][Bb]
![Ii][Dd]_[Ee][Dd]25519.[Pp][Uu][Bb]
![Ii][Dd]_[Ee][Cc][Dd][Ss][Aa].[Pp][Uu][Bb]
![Ii][Dd]_[Dd][Ss][Aa].[Pp][Uu][Bb]
